The desk merged every "wrong date" report into [#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) because the subject
lines matched ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). The subjects match. The
bugs don't. Split by tenant, by how the report came in and by what the reporter says moved, the
reports fall into three problems. The parser fix that's queued covers one of them.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

<Callout kind="warn">
Shipping the parser fix will not close #4471. The other two problems have different causes and
need their own fixes.
</Callout>

## `parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first

Every tenant in #4471 writes dates day first. The shared parser reads every date month first, so
8 June comes back as 6 August. This is the problem the incident page describes, and the queued
fix is for it. It reaches every way in except the booking-v3 form: the app, email, and the
booking-v2 form, whose hint already says `DD/MM/YYYY`.

Ticket [#{tickets.parser.number}]({tickets.parser.url}).

## `hint`: the booking-v3 form tells people to type the month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what it says. The form then
reads the date the tenant's way, day first, and the booking lands with day and month swapped. The
symptom looks exactly like `parser`, but fixing the parser won't change it. The hint is wrong,
so the hint has to change: it should follow the tenant's locale.

Only reports that came in through the form, at a tenant on booking-v3, belong here.

Ticket [#{tickets.hint.number}]({tickets.hint.url}).

## `one-day`: a date that moves by one day

These reports describe no swap. "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday 11 May." "Client
in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting." The date shifts by a day, and
it shifts differently for people in different places. That reads like a time zone bug, a date
stored as midnight in one zone and shown in another. Day order has nothing to do with it, so
neither fix above moves these back.

The desk already spotted them (`desk-notes.json`), but the incident page counts them under
`parser` and puts them in a footnote. They get their own ticket here.

Ticket [#{oneDay.number}]({oneDay.url}).

## Telling them apart

1. Read what moved. Day and month swapped means `parser` or `hint`. One day off means `one-day`.
2. For a swap, check how the report came in. Through the form at a booking-v3 tenant is `hint`.
   Anything else is `parser`.
3. Ignore the intake tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser, not the tenant, and the
   table shows how often it guesses wrong.
4. Don't merge on subject. The same subject lines turn up under different problems.

